{"reply":"In Chapter 5, the Caterpillar turns Alice’s uncertainty into an examination: “So you think you’re changed, do you?” He keeps asking her to account for what has changed, then tests her by asking her to repeat “You are old, Father William.” Her answers expose how difficult it is for her to give a stable account of herself. [Chapter 5 — *Advice from a Caterpillar*]

A close philosophical parallel is Nicolas Zaks’s account of the Socratic elenchus, or refutation. He describes questioning as a way of placing someone’s opinions side by side until their conflicts become visible; the resulting perplexity can interrupt a false confidence in knowing. [Zaks, “Socratic Elenchus in the Sophist”](https://doi.org/10.1515/apeiron-2017-0064)

The connection is structural, not literal: the Caterpillar is not necessarily practicing a formal Socratic method. But both encounters make confusion do more than signal failure. They turn it into a pressure that reveals an unstable assumption—in Alice’s case, the assumption that she can still explain who she is in familiar terms. Does reading her confusion as the beginning of inquiry fit what you noticed in the exchange?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln1004-1056","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 1004-1056","exact_quote":"“So you think you’re changed, do you?”","supported_claims":["In Chapter 5, the Caterpillar turns Alice’s uncertainty into an examination: “So you think you’re changed, do you?” He keeps asking her to account for what has changed, then tests her by asking her to repeat “You are old, Father William.” Her answers expose how difficult it is for her to give a stable account of herself."]},{"source_kind":"web","evidence_id":"https://doi.org/10.1515/apeiron-2017-0064","exact_quote":null,"supported_claims":["A close philosophical parallel is Nicolas Zaks’s account of the Socratic elenchus, or refutation. He describes questioning as a way of placing someone’s opinions side by side until their conflicts become visible; the resulting perplexity can interrupt a false confidence in knowing.","The connection is structural, not literal: the Caterpillar is not necessarily practicing a formal Socratic method. But both encounters make confusion do more than signal failure. They turn it into a pressure that reveals an unstable assumption—in Alice’s case, the assumption that she can still explain who she is in familiar terms."]}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}
